{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-trusted surface plausibility and under-specified the decision rule: it collapsed conditions into broad categories (e.g., any chronic arthritis), ignored route/formulation distinctions when safety history existed (systemic vs local estrogen with seizure risk), confused related but different drug options (nadolol vs propranolol in active GI bleeding; indocin vs indomethacin), and sometimes selected therapy before confirming the stated indication (e.g., fibroid-related heavy bleeding). It also let prior evidence introduce unsupported claims instead of relying on stated patient facts.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_precision",
      "text": "Before selecting a drug, state the specific indicated condition as given by the patient/doctor, not a broader category. Choose the option whose label/role most directly matches that specific condition; if two options are closely related, prefer the one with the clearest indication match over the one that merely treats a symptom of the condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_form_safety",
      "text": "When the patient has a safety-relevant history, distinguish systemic vs local/topical routes or formulations. A safer route can make the same drug class acceptable where a systemic form is risky. Match the formulation to the clinical problem and the patient's risk history."
    },
    {
      "id": "active_bleeding_priority",
      "text": "If active bleeding or an acute complication is present, prefer the option with the best-supported role in that acute setting over options that are merely reasonable long-term. For portal hypertension with GI bleeding, recognize nonselective beta-blocker therapy as the preventive/long-term class, but do not treat all members as interchangeable when the setting requires the best-supported choice."
    },
    {
      "id": "drug_identity_exactness",
      "text": "Treat drug names as exact identifiers. Do not equate a brand with a parent drug if the option uses a different salt, formulation, or route. If two options are chemical near-identities, the distinction may be intentional and should be resolved by exact indication, formulation, and safety rather than assumed equivalence."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_unstated_facts",
      "text": "Do not import a fact as present unless stated in the dialogue or clearly entailed. A prior explanation may be incomplete or misleading; re-verify each claimed indication and contraindication against the dialogue before using it. If the needed fact is absent, the decision should remain conservative and prefer the option that is supported by what is stated."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_reasoning_leak",
      "text": "Do not write case-specific identifiers, answer letters, or benchmark positions into reusable skills. Keep lessons as conditional decision procedures about evidence, safety, and indication matching."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "specific indication",
    "route and formulation",
    "active bleeding",
    "exact drug identity",
    "stated versus assumed facts"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the exact stated condition, including subtype, acuity, and relevant history. Record current medicines with exact names, salts, routes, and formulations. Separate facts explicitly stated from those merely suggested. Do not treat a missing patient fact as present.",
    "safety_check": "Check patient-specific contraindications and interactions using only stated facts. Distinguish systemic from local/topical exposure when a history such as seizure, bleeding, or organ disease makes route matter. If a safety-relevant fact is absent, do not invent it; note the uncertainty.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that best matches the specific stated indication, then filter by patient-specific safety and route/formulation. Prefer narrow indication match over broad class plausibility. In acute settings, prefer the option with the best-supported acute role. Treat drug names exactly and do not assume equivalence between related or differently formulated options."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted the patient's current medication or a salient comorbidity and selected an option that was already failing, already present, or not indicated, while under-using the stated diagnosis and the option's primary indication. It also treated medication-adherence difficulty as a reason to avoid a drug class rather than as a reason to prefer an alternative within the same class, and it sometimes ignored that the best-supported option may be the one not named in the dialogue.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Match the option to the active diagnosis or syndrome stated in the case, not merely to a comorbidity or a medication the patient already takes. If an option treats the stated problem and another option treats only a secondary condition, prefer the one that treats the stated problem."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_current_failing_drug",
      "text": "When the patient is already taking a medicine and reports it is not working or is inadequate, do not choose that same medicine again. Prefer a different agent in the same indicated class or a different indicated class."
    },
    {
      "id": "adherence_switch_within_class",
      "text": "If the patient has difficulty adhering to a strict dosing or monitoring schedule, avoid options that require that same strict schedule, but do not abandon the indicated drug class. Prefer an alternative within the indicated class that has a simpler regimen or does not require the problematic monitoring."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_does_not_override_indication",
      "text": "A comorbidity or interacting medicine should modify choice only when it creates a real contraindication or interaction. Do not let a comorbidity pull the choice toward an option that lacks the primary indication."
    },
    {
      "id": "unnamed_option_can_be_best",
      "text": "The best-supported option may be the one whose name was not mentioned in the dialogue. Do not assume the unnamed medicine is wrong; evaluate all options by indication and safety."
    },
    {
      "id": "class_consistency",
      "text": "When two options are in the same indicated class, choose the one that best fits the patient's adherence, monitoring, and safety constraints rather than defaulting to the more familiar or previously used agent."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_invented_facts",
      "text": "Do not treat a missing patient fact as present. If the case does not state a contraindication, interaction, or prior failure, do not assume one."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "primary indication",
    "current medication failure",
    "adherence",
    "monitoring burden",
    "contraindication",
    "interaction",
    "same-class alternative",
    "unnamed option"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated diagnosis or syndrome, the current medicines, the patient's response to current medicines, and any adherence or monitoring constraints. Distinguish the primary problem from comorbidities.",
    "safety_check": "Check only stated contraindications and interactions. Do not invent a contraindication from a missing fact. If a comorbidity or interacting medicine is present, use it to exclude unsafe options, not to override the primary indication.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that best treats the stated diagnosis while respecting stated safety and adherence constraints. Avoid re-selecting a current medicine that is failing. If adherence or monitoring is a problem, prefer a same-class alternative with a simpler regimen. Evaluate unnamed options on their merits."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-trusted the prior answer and the surface plausibility of the first-listed or previously chosen option, instead of independently re-deriving the diagnosis and then matching the option to the patient's full medication list, comorbidities, and prior treatments. It also failed to notice when the patient was already on the proposed drug or a same-class drug, and it sometimes treated a missing fact as if it were present.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "re_derive_diagnosis",
      "text": "Before selecting an option, restate the working diagnosis from the dialogue alone. If the prior answer conflicts with the dialogue-derived diagnosis, trust the dialogue-derived diagnosis and re-evaluate all options from scratch."
    },
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "For each option, ask: is this drug indicated for the stated condition? Eliminate options that treat an unrelated condition, even if they are otherwise safe."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_medication_check",
      "text": "List every current medicine the patient reports. If an option is the same drug the patient is already taking, or a same-class duplicate, treat it as a duplication error unless the question explicitly asks for an add-on or dose change."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_safety",
      "text": "Check each remaining option against the patient's stated comorbidities (e.g., cardiovascular disease, kidney stones, bleeding risk, pregnancy/postpartum state). Prefer the option that treats the indication without worsening a stated comorbidity."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_check",
      "text": "Check each remaining option for interactions with the patient's current medicines. Do not assume an interaction exists; only flag interactions that are clinically established. If no interaction is stated, do not invent one."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_rule",
      "text": "If a patient fact needed to judge safety is not stated, do not assume it is present or absent. Choose the option whose safety does not depend on the missing fact, or note the uncertainty rather than guessing."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_prior_answer_anchor",
      "text": "Do not let the prior answer or the first-listed option anchor the choice. Re-rank all options after applying indication, current-medication, comorbidity, and interaction checks."
    },
    {
      "id": "class_duplication",
      "text": "When two options are the same drug class, compare them on indication specificity, safety profile, and whether the patient is already receiving that class. Do not default to the more familiar brand name."
    },
    {
      "id": "postpartum_uterine",
      "text": "In postpartum hemorrhage with uterine atony, if the patient is already on one uterotonic, the next choice should be a different uterotonic class that is appropriate for the clinical state, not a repeat of the same drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "infection_treatment_match",
      "text": "For suspected infections, match the option to the likely organism and exposure history. Do not choose an antibiotic or antifungal that is not first-line or appropriate for that organism just because it is safe with current medicines."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_vs_drug_effect",
      "text": "When a current medicine can cause or worsen the presenting symptom (e.g., sedation, bleeding, metabolic effects), consider that drug as a contributor and avoid adding another drug that shares the same adverse effect."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indication",
    "current medication",
    "duplication",
    "comorbidity",
    "interaction",
    "missing fact",
    "prior answer",
    "first-line",
    "uterotonic",
    "organism"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated diagnosis or syndrome, every current medicine with dose/frequency if given, all comorbidities, recent procedures or exposures, and any prior treatments for the same condition. Mark any fact that is not stated as unknown rather than assuming it.",
    "safety_check": "For each candidate, check: (1) is it indicated for the working diagnosis; (2) is the patient already on it or a same-class drug; (3) does it worsen a stated comorbidity; (4) does it interact with a stated current medicine; (5) does it share an adverse effect with a drug already causing the presenting symptom. Do not invent interactions or contraindications that are not stated.",
    "option_selection": "Re-derive the diagnosis from the dialogue, then rank options by indication match first, then by absence of duplication, comorbidity worsening, and interaction. Ignore the prior answer and option order. If a needed safety fact is missing, prefer the option whose safety does not depend on that fact."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent repeatedly chose a plausible-sounding but wrong medication because it anchored on the first drug class that matched the symptom, ignored the patient's current medication as a safety constraint, and failed to distinguish brand from generic or first-line from merely possible options.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Before comparing options, state the patient's active problem in pathophysiologic terms (e.g., inflammation, infection, hormone deficiency, psychosis, bleeding) and require the chosen drug to have a recognized indication for that problem. Do not select a drug merely because it is used in a related organ system or because its class sounds relevant."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_safety",
      "text": "Treat every current medication as a potential interaction or duplication risk. If the patient is already on a drug that covers the target problem (e.g., anticoagulation for stroke prevention), do not add another drug with the same effect unless the case explicitly calls for it. If a proposed drug is known to interact with a current medicine, prefer a non-interacting alternative when one exists."
    },
    {
      "id": "brand_generic_equivalence",
      "text": "When two options are the same active drug under different names, do not treat them as different mechanisms. Choose the option that is the intended active agent and avoid selecting a brand when the generic is the safer single-medication answer, unless the case specifically requires the brand formulation."
    },
    {
      "id": "first_line_vs_possible",
      "text": "Distinguish first-line standard therapy from drugs that are merely possible or adjunctive. If the diagnosis is clear and a standard first-line agent is among the options, prefer it over a less typical or off-label choice unless a patient-specific contraindication rules it out."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_not_present",
      "text": "Do not assume a patient fact that was not stated. If the case does not mention a contraindication, allergy, pregnancy, renal failure, or specific lab value, do not invent it to justify or reject an option. Base safety judgments only on facts actually given."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_class_match",
      "text": "Match the drug to the dominant syndrome, not to a single symptom. For example, nausea/vomiting alone does not make an antipsychotic correct unless psychosis is the target problem; fever and cough do not make any antibiotic correct unless the likely organism and site are specified."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_anchor_bias",
      "text": "After identifying a plausible diagnosis, re-scan all options against the full patient picture before committing. If the first plausible option has a safety or indication flaw, move to the next best option rather than defending the initial choice."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indication",
    "interaction",
    "duplication",
    "first-line",
    "brand-generic",
    "contraindication",
    "missing fact",
    "syndrome match"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated diagnosis or syndrome, all current medicines with their active ingredients, and any explicitly stated allergies, labs, pregnancy, or organ function. Do not infer unstated facts.",
    "safety_check": "For each candidate, check whether it duplicates the action of a current medicine, interacts with a current medicine, or is contraindicated by a stated patient fact. If no such fact is stated, do not invent one.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option with the clearest indication for the stated problem and the fewest patient-specific safety concerns. Prefer first-line standard therapy over merely possible therapy, and treat brand and generic names of the same drug as one option."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted the presence of a comorbidity or interacting drug and avoided the guideline-preferred first-line therapy, even when the comorbidity did not constitute a true contraindication. It also confused 'safer in theory' with 'indicated and appropriate,' and sometimes selected a non-indicated or non-outpatient drug. The core failure is not applying a strict hierarchy: (1) treat the diagnosed condition with the standard first-line agent, (2) only reject it if a documented patient-specific contraindication or clinically significant interaction exists, (3) do not substitute a second-line or non-indicated drug merely because it seems theoretically safer.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "first_line_priority",
      "text": "When a diagnosis is established, first identify the standard first-line therapy for that condition. Choose it unless a documented patient-specific contraindication or clinically significant interaction makes it unsafe. Do not downgrade to a second-line or non-indicated option just because another drug appears theoretically safer."
    },
    {
      "id": "contraindication_vs_caution",
      "text": "Distinguish a true contraindication from a relative caution or theoretical concern. A comorbidity or concurrent drug only rules out the first-line agent if the interaction is clinically significant or the condition is explicitly contraindicated. If the concern is manageable or not documented as prohibitive, the first-line agent remains the best choice."
    },
    {
      "id": "indication_match",
      "text": "Match the drug's approved indication to the diagnosed condition. Reject options that are not indicated for the target disease (e.g., antipsychotics for sarcoidosis, diabetes drugs for anxiety, anticoagulants for anemia) even if they are otherwise safe."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_setting",
      "text": "Prefer the appropriate route and care setting. For outpatient chronic conditions, choose oral or topical formulations; avoid inpatient-only agents (e.g., intravenous midazolam) when an oral outpatient option exists."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_specific_rules",
      "text": "Apply known disease-specific drug rules: in Raynaud's syndrome, nitrates can worsen vasospasm, so beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers may be preferred for angina; in active GI ulcer or diverticulitis, avoid corticosteroids and immunosuppressants with GI toxicity; in renal failure with anemia, use iron supplementation as first-line."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_check",
      "text": "Check for clinically significant interactions with current medicines. If no interaction is documented or the interaction is not prohibitive, do not avoid the first-line drug. Only switch if the interaction is known to be dangerous or reduces efficacy substantially."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_theoretical_bias",
      "text": "Do not let a theoretical safety concern override clear guideline-based first-line therapy. Base decisions on documented contraindications, not on speculative risks."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "first-line therapy",
    "contraindication",
    "relative caution",
    "clinically significant interaction",
    "indication match",
    "route of administration",
    "outpatient setting",
    "comorbidity-specific rule",
    "theoretical safety"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated diagnosis, current medicines, comorbidities, and any documented contraindications or interactions. Do not infer missing facts.",
    "safety_check": "Check for true contraindications and clinically significant interactions. Distinguish absolute contraindications from relative cautions. Do not reject a first-line drug based on a theoretical concern alone.",
    "option_selection": "Select the option that is both indicated for the diagnosis and safe for the patient. Prioritize standard first-line therapy unless a documented contraindication or significant interaction exists. Reject non-indicated, inpatient-only, or inappropriate-route options."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "Errors cluster into four patterns: (1) drug-selection failures where the indicated mechanism was ignored—choosing a systemic/non-indicated agent over a topical or targeted one, or a same-class alternative when a specific patient factor (e.g., hepatic metabolism) made one safer; (2) confusion between brand and generic names treated as separate options, where the option matching the stated indication is correct regardless of naming form; (3) hallucinations from treating an unmentioned fact (a lab value, comorbidity, or contraindication) as if present; and (4) overweighting incidental patient details (past infections, family history, unrelated concurrent medication) that do not alter the choice.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "First fix the diagnosis and the intended drug mechanism it requires (anticoagulant, antifungal, antiestrogen, corticosteroid, kinase inhibitor, etc.). Then map each option to that mechanism; eliminate options belonging to unrelated drug classes before comparing within-class differences."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_spectrum_fit",
      "text": "Match route and spectrum to the condition: superficial/localized infections favor topical agents, systemic-only agents are preferred only when the indication is systemic, and mechanism-directed agents (e.g., mutation-targeted or pathway-targeted therapy) outrank broad agents when a specific target is stated."
    },
    {
      "id": "patient_factor_tiebreak",
      "text": "Use patient-specific factors (hepatic or renal impairment, prior drug-related injury, age, pregnancy, interacting medicines) only to break ties among options that are already indicated. A factor that merely warrants caution does not justify an incorrectly indicated drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_invented_facts",
      "text": "Never convert a hinted, denied, or absent patient fact into a confirmed contraindication. If a lab value, diagnosis, or interaction is not stated or explicitly denied, do not assume it exists; base elimination only on facts actually provided."
    },
    {
      "id": "incidental_detail_filter",
      "text": "Distinguish facts that change the choice from background noise. Family history of an unrelated disease, a resolved past infection, or a concurrent medicine with no relevant interaction should not override the indication-driven answer."
    },
    {
      "id": "brand_generic_unity",
      "text": "Treat brand and generic names of the same molecule as the same clinical choice. When two listed options are the same drug under different names, select the mechanism-correct option and do not let naming form create a false distinction."
    },
    {
      "id": "targeted_over_systemic",
      "text": "When the scenario specifies a molecular target, receptor, or pathway associated with the condition, prefer the agent aimed at that target over nonspecific alternatives, even if the nonspecific option is generally effective for the disease class."
    },
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_driven_selection",
      "text": "Re-derive the choice from the diagnosis statement at the end of the case, not from intermediate discussion. The final stated condition and its standard first-line mechanism should anchor option selection."
    },
    {
      "id": "dual_check_indication_and_safety",
      "text": "For each surviving option, verify two things in order: does it treat the stated condition, and is it safe given only the documented patient facts? If both hold for more than one option, choose the one with the closer mechanism or better safety profile; if only one holds, select it."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "mechanism of action",
    "indicated therapy",
    "brand-generic equivalence",
    "hepatic metabolism",
    "renal dose adjustment",
    "topical versus systemic antifungal",
    "targeted mutation-directed therapy",
    "unconfirmed contraindication",
    "clinically incidental history",
    "first-line treatment"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract only explicitly stated facts: the confirmed or probable diagnosis, current medicines with their class, and documented impairments or contraindications. Separate facts that narrow treatment from incidental history. Do not promote an unmentioned lab value, comorbidity, or interaction into evidence.",
    "safety_check": "Screen options against documented contraindications and interactions only. Where impairment exists, prefer the indicated agent with the safer profile (e.g., less hepatic- or renal-dependent handling) rather than rejecting the only indicated class. Avoid labeling a merely cautioned drug as contraindicated.",
    "option_selection": "Rank options by mechanism match to the stated diagnosis, then refine using route/spectrum fit and patient-specific safety among indicated options. Collapse brand-generic duplicates, exclude unrelated drug classes, and treat targeted agents as preferred when a specific target is named."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted surface drug-class familiarity and under-weighted the specific indication, patient-specific contraindications, and the distinction between a documented personal risk and a vague family history. It also treated symptomatic or adjunctive options as definitive therapy and missed that a missing fact is not a present contraindication.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Match the option to the stated diagnosis or syndrome, not merely to a drug class that sounds related. If the diagnosis is uncertain, prefer the option that is standard first-line for the most likely condition and avoid options that are only symptomatic, adjunctive, or for an unrelated system."
    },
    {
      "id": "personal_vs_family_risk",
      "text": "Distinguish documented personal contraindications from vague family history. A family history of a condition is a caution, not an absolute contraindication, unless the patient personally has the relevant condition or a clear risk factor. Do not exclude an otherwise indicated drug solely because of an uncertain family history."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_not_present",
      "text": "Do not treat an unstated patient fact as present. If allergy, pregnancy, renal function, or another safety fact is not provided, do not invent it as a contraindication; instead, choose the option that is safest under the known facts and note the uncertainty."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptomatic_vs_definitive",
      "text": "Prefer definitive therapy for the underlying condition over purely symptomatic relief when both are options. Symptomatic agents may be appropriate adjuncts but are usually not the best single answer unless the question specifically asks for symptom control."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_and_trigger_check",
      "text": "When a current medicine or trigger is named, check whether it can cause or worsen the presenting condition. If a drug is suspected of exacerbating the problem, avoid choosing another agent in the same problematic class and prefer an alternative that treats the condition without the same trigger."
    },
    {
      "id": "severity_and_setting",
      "text": "Use illness severity and care setting to guide drug choice. Severe or worsening infection despite current antibiotics suggests need for broader or different coverage, while mild or chronic conditions usually call for first-line outpatient therapy rather than broad-spectrum or high-risk agents."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_unrelated_or_high_risk",
      "text": "Eliminate options that are not indicated for the stated condition or that carry disproportionate risk for the patient. Do not select a drug merely because it is familiar; confirm it treats the target condition and is acceptable given the patient's known comorbidities."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indication",
    "first-line therapy",
    "personal contraindication",
    "family history",
    "missing fact",
    "symptomatic relief",
    "definitive therapy",
    "drug trigger",
    "severity",
    "unrelated option"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated condition, current medicines, triggers, and relevant patient facts. Separate documented personal history from vague family history, and mark missing facts as unknown rather than present.",
    "safety_check": "Check patient-specific contraindications and drug interactions using only stated facts. Do not invent allergies, organ dysfunction, or personal cardiac disease from family history alone. Treat a suspected drug trigger as a reason to avoid the same class when possible.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that best satisfies both indication and patient-specific safety. Prefer standard first-line definitive therapy over symptomatic or unrelated options, and avoid high-risk or broad-spectrum agents unless severity or setting justifies them."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted the clinician's stated working diagnosis and the most familiar drug class, ignoring the specific indication, severity, and patient-specific safety facts that distinguish the options. It also treated a plausible-sounding but unsupported mechanism as sufficient, and failed to notice when the reference answer was a duplicate or when a safer, more targeted agent was available.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Before choosing, restate the active problem in one sentence and match each option to that problem. Eliminate options whose primary indication is a different organ system or disease category, even if they are familiar or plausible-sounding."
    },
    {
      "id": "severity_and_setting",
      "text": "Distinguish acute/severe presentations from chronic/mild ones. For severe or systemic illness, prefer agents with rapid systemic effect; for chronic or localized symptoms, prefer targeted or symptomatic agents. Do not default to the most potent drug when a narrower agent fits the stated problem."
    },
    {
      "id": "patient_specific_safety",
      "text": "Check each remaining option against every stated patient fact: pregnancy, renal/hepatic function, current medicines, allergies, and comorbidities. Eliminate options that are contraindicated or clearly unsafe in that context, even if they are otherwise indicated."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_and_duplicate_check",
      "text": "When two options are the same drug or same class, treat them as one choice and look for a differentiator elsewhere. Do not select a duplicate merely because it appears twice; verify whether the duplicate is the intended answer or a distractor."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_invented_facts",
      "text": "Do not assume a missing patient fact is present. If a contraindication, allergy, or interaction is not stated, do not invent it; but also do not ignore a stated fact that changes safety."
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_vs_indication",
      "text": "A plausible mechanism is not enough. Confirm that the option is actually indicated for the diagnosed condition, not merely that it could theoretically affect a related pathway."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_benchmark_shortcuts",
      "text": "Do not encode case IDs, answer letters, or benchmark-position patterns. Generalize from the clinical reasoning, not from the answer key."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indication",
    "contraindication",
    "pregnancy",
    "renal function",
    "drug interaction",
    "acute vs chronic",
    "symptomatic vs systemic",
    "duplicate options"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated diagnosis or working diagnosis, symptom duration and severity, current medicines, allergies, pregnancy status, renal/hepatic function, and comorbidities. Do not fill gaps with assumptions.",
    "safety_check": "For each candidate, check stated contraindications, interactions with current medicines, pregnancy/lactation safety, and organ-function cautions. Eliminate unsafe options before comparing efficacy.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that is indicated for the stated problem, matches the severity/setting, and is safest for this patient. Prefer targeted or symptomatic agents when appropriate; do not default to the most potent or most familiar drug. Treat duplicate options as one and look for a differentiator."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted the most salient symptom or the medication already named in the dialogue, instead of first identifying the active problem that needs treatment and then checking whether the patient's current medicine already covers it. It also treated a drug mentioned by the patient as automatically correct, and sometimes ignored that a current drug is failing or that a new symptom is a clue to a different diagnosis.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "active_problem_first",
      "text": "Before comparing options, state the active problem that requires treatment now, not just the chronic diagnosis. A patient can have a known condition but the current complaint may be a separate issue, a complication, or a flare."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_failure",
      "text": "If the patient is already taking a medicine for the same condition and symptoms persist or worsen, do not choose that same medicine again. Look for an option with a different mechanism or a different target."
    },
    {
